16.3.1. Assignment Description The Assignment command enables you to assign values to created variables. PolyScope X Software Handbook Copyright © 2009–2026 by Universal Robots A/S. All rights reserved. To access Assignment command 2. Tap the Assignment icon in the Commands toolbox. A node with three editable fields is inserted into the program tree: • Variable • Value Type • Expression 3. Tap the Variable field to choose your variable: • grid • grid_iterator • var The Variable field contains the name of the variable. By default, the variable is called var. Software Handbook PolyScope X Copyright © 2009–2026 by Universal Robots A/S. All rights reserved. 4. Tap the Value Type field to and select your choice: • Array • Boolean • Float • Grid • Integer • Matrix • Pose • String • Timer • Waypoint The Value Type contains the type of variable you are creating. Type Allowed Values/Examples Examples Array A list of values Boolean True/False True Float A decimal point value 1.23 Grid A grid defined in the application Integer A whole number 5 Matrix Pose A pose for the robot to move p[0,0,0,0,0,0,] String Text “Hello World” Waypoint A waypoint is defined in the program 5. Tap the Expression field, select your variables and expression, and Confirm. The Expression is the value to assign the variable. See Expression Editor. Note: Tap the icon arrow left and arrow right in the right sidebar of the main screen if you want the assignment to be reverted. PolyScope X Software Handbook Copyright © 2009–2026 by Universal Robots A/S. All rights reserved.

**Source 7 (documentation):**
12. Value-Variable-Expression Tabs Description The Value, Variable, Expression tabs appear as pop-up dialog in opening commands. They are common components used in almost all commands. Value tab description The Value tab is used when you know the exact fixed value of a program. It restricts you to values appropriate for the given variable. The Value Type drop-down tab in the Assignment command contains the type of variable you are creating and has one of the following values: Type Allowed Values/Examples Examples Array A list of values Boolean True/False True Float A decimal point value 1.23 Grid A grid defined in the application Integer A whole number 5 Matrix Pose A pose for the robot to move p[0,0,0,0,0,0,] String Text “Hello World” Waypoint A waypoint is defined in the program PolyScope X Software Handbook Copyright © 2009–2026 by Universal Robots A/S. All rights reserved. Example use of Value tab Create a restrictive value appropriate for a given variable, which runs five times.

**Source 8 (documentation):**
These are the different variable types and their corresponding examples: • Integer: numbers [1, 3.14159] • Float: numbers [3, 4.24261] • Boolean: True, False • String: “Next part” • Matrix: [[1, 0, 0], [0, 1, 0], [0, 0, 1]] • Pose: p[0.1, 0, 0.3, 3.14, 0, 0] • Array: array of numbers, strings, poses - [3, 44, 55, 66, 77], ["Start position"], [p[0, 0, 0, 3.14, 0, 0], p[0.3, -0.1, 0.5, 0, 1.57, -0.33]] PolyScope X Software Handbook Copyright © 2009–2026 by Universal Robots A/S. All rights reserved. 20.6.2. Delete Variables Deleting variables in programs Tap the trash button to remove a variable. Removing a variable is possible only when a program is stopped. Program will be stopped automatically if it's still running. NOTICE Type and name cannot be changed after variable is added. Value can be changed when application is stopped. Value is also updated live when program is running. Software Handbook PolyScope X Copyright © 2009–2026 by Universal Robots A/S. All rights reserved. NOTICE Structs, which are complex data types, are not supported in application variables. Communication handler objects (e.g. xmlrpc, ros_subscriber, etc.) are not supported as application variables. Application variables can be used in the same way as any other variables in the program. They are added to a list of variables available in the dropdown lists. NOTICE See the variables inside the blue box in both the main screen and the multitask screen. Information found in both screens correspond to each other. PolyScope X Software Handbook Copyright © 2009–2026 by Universal Robots A/S. All rights reserved.

**Source 9 (documentation):**
7. Tap Joint Move command. Note that in PolyScope X, the Waypoint node is merged to this command. Two editable fields, two tabs, and an ellipses icon are inserted into the program tree: • Motion Profile • Name • Edit Point • Move Here Software Handbook PolyScope X Copyright © 2009–2026 by Universal Robots A/S. All rights reserved. 8. Tap the Edit Point tab. The screen with the robot arm in 3D appears. To the right of the screen, you see the Move menu. 9. Use the plus-minus button of the X, Y, Z axes to jog the robot and set the position of your waypoint and Save. Name the first waypoint 10. In the Name field, enter Point_A as the name in the Value editor tab and Confirm. PolyScope X Software Handbook Copyright © 2009–2026 by Universal Robots A/S. All rights reserved. 13. A Signal field is added. Select DO 0. A Value field is added to the right and select High. Insert Waiting time 18. In the Signal field, select DO 0. A Value field is added to the right and select Low. Move to Position B 19. Tap the add icon in the program tree. Repeat steps 7 to 10 to create a second waypoint. Move the robot arm position in a direction clearly distinguished from Point_A. Software Handbook PolyScope X Copyright © 2009–2026 by Universal Robots A/S. All rights reserved. Name the second waypoint 20. In the Name field, enter Point_B as the name in the Value editor tab and Confirm. The main screen shows you the first created program. Verify program in 3D viewer and Communication application 22. On the footer, tap the Play button. When you are in the program tree screen, the program in green color signifies that it is the current program execution.

